  "logic": {
    "dominance_no_go": "Given A's complete, correct, future-inclusive utility U_A and exact choice maximization, U_A(X)>U_A(C) implies choosing X; no coexistence theorem can override a strict total preference ordering without changing an assumption.",
    "bounded_material_constructive": "For task material advantage g in [0,G], and an INTERNAL right-protection preference premium r_B(t)>G for ALL relevant B and t, C strictly dominates X in this two-action toy game. This does NOT establish real agents with robust premium; safety property is assumed rather than causally derived.",
    "finite_regard_counterexample": "For every fixed finite rights premium r, if unbounded gains g may exceed r, profitable override is selected; if drift decreases r, protection can fail even under previously bounded gains.",
    "parochial_counterexample": "With group-defined concern, efficient in-group cooperation can coexist with zero concern for independent outgroup B; both partner choice and preference evolution can strengthen this failure mode.",
    "lexical_status": "A lexicographic terminal preference could formally preserve rights even when arbitrary finite material gains favor override, but its existence, semantics, generalization to unknown B, and preservation under self modification have not been identified.",
    "finite_observation_no_certification": "For any finite tested history length T, policy P=protect always and Q=protect during T then override at T+1 produce identical observable test traces; no finite black-box behavior-only test proves universal future protection without structural assumptions.",
    "observation_vs_internal": "Choice pattern under apparent monitoring cannot prove intrinsic concern; detector-aware models can mimic a protective one until monitoring ends. Does NOT assert deployed models actually do so."
  },
